# Odoo v15 Module Development Guidelines for ACCON Modules
- If you are creating a new module, create the folder first and later the files inside it

## Module Structure
accon_module_name/
├── __init__.py
├── __manifest__.py
├── controllers/
│   ├── __init__.py
│   └── controllers.py
├── data/
│   └── module_data.xml
├── demo/
│   └── demo_data.xml
├── i18n/
│   └── en_US.po
├── models/
│   ├── __init__.py
│   └── models.py
├── security/
│   ├── ir.model.access.csv
│   └── security_rules.xml
├── static/
│   ├── description/
│   │   └── icon.png
│   └── src/
├── views/
│   ├── templates.xml
│   └── views.xml
└── wizards/
    ├── __init__.py
    └── wizards.py

## Naming Conventions
- Module Name: Always start with `accon_` (e.g., accon_invoicing)
- Model Names: 
  * Model name: accon.model.name  
  * Table name: accon_model_name
  * Class name: AcconModelName
- Field Names: snake_case (invoice_line_id, employee_code)
- Method Names: snake_case (compute_total, validate_invoice)
- XML IDs: accon_module.model_name_action

## Manifest File
- version: '15.0.1.0.0'
- Follow proper dependency ordering
- List security files first in data
- Make a module description very clear, with concrete and direct plain words so everyone can understand but highlight important technical details

## Model Definitions
- Use proper inheritance
- Make fields trackable when appropriate
- Use computed fields with proper depends
- Add helpful docstrings

## View Definitions
- Consistent naming: accon_model_name_view_form
- Use statusbar_visible for main states
- Apply proper decorations in list view

## Security
- Always define proper access rights
- Consider record rules for multi-company
- Make everyone be able to read, delete, modify and create by default unless it is specified
- Don´t leave spaces or similar that could lead to errors

## Best Practices
- Follow semantic versioning
- One class per file when possible
- Use proper indexing for search fields
- Add translation markers _()
- Write unit tests for important functionality
- Document complex logic